# NMesh web console

A local management surface for one node: a web page to watch the network
(graph, peers, throughput, load) and to drive the node (invitations, joining a
network, trusting new certificates).

This is **the most sensitive surface in the project** — whoever controls the
console controls the node. It is therefore built paranoid and **with no external
dependency** (stdlib + `cryptography`, already present).

## Running it

```bash
python scripts/console_demo.py --data ./mydata
```

On the first run the password is **generated and printed once** — write it down.
The SHA-256 fingerprint of the self-signed TLS certificate is printed too, so it
can be checked in the browser.

Options:
- `--console-host 0.0.0.0`: makes the console reachable from another machine. By
  default it listens on `127.0.0.1` only.
- `--console-port 8787`, `--no-tls`, `--data DIR` (persists identity + creds).

## Navigation

Four sections, in the order you need them: what the node is doing, who it talks
to, what runs on it, what you can change. **Every section is a route** —
`#section/subsection` — so it is linkable, bookmarkable, and the browser's Back
button works.

| Section | Subsections | Route |
|---|---|---|
| **Overview** | — | `#overview` |
| **Network** | Peers · Reachability · Add a node | `#network/peers`, `#network/reach`, `#network/join` |
| **Apps** | Installed · Find an app | `#apps/installed`, `#apps/store` |
| **Settings** | Identity · Updates · Security · This browser · Configuration · Diagnostics · Advanced | `#settings/identity`, `#settings/updates`, `#settings/security`, `#settings/appearance`, `#settings/config`, `#settings/diagnostics`, `#settings/advanced` |

Joining a network is a **network** action, not a setting: tickets, QR codes,
manual exchange, relays and certificates all live in *Network → Add a node*.

**Nodes and links are two counts, never one.** A node may hold several links at
once — a LAN address and a punched UDP path, say — so "connected to 2 nodes" and
"3 links up" are both true at the same time. Every number on screen names which
one it is: the Overview headline and the **Network** tab badge count *nodes*;
the rail line, the *Active links* card and each transport's badge count *links*.
They come from two separate fields (`node_count`, `link_count`), because one
field under two labels is a field that is wrong under one of them.

**⌘K / Ctrl-K** opens a command palette: any section, plus the common actions
(ping, create a ticket, check for updates, switch theme). **The theme** follows
the system by default; the top bar's button picks light or dark, and the choice
stays in this browser.

Appearance, tokens and components are described in
[`Docs/WebConsole/design`](design) — read it **before** touching a page.

### Auto-refresh (top bar)

The console re-reads the node on a timer, and **you set that timer**. On a
desktop it is a number of seconds next to the ⟳ button: `0` turns it off, `30`
is the maximum. Under 720 px the field becomes a short list (off · 1s · 5s ·
10s · 30s) — a number spinner is a poor target on a phone, and a list of five
answers covers what anyone actually picks. A value typed on a keyboard that is
not in the list is added to it rather than silently rounded to one that is. The
choice is kept per browser (`localStorage`), never sent to the node, and ⟳
still reads the node once when auto-refresh is off.

**The contract a refresh must honour**: it updates *values*. It does not close
an open dialog or menu, does not drop a selected row, and does not wipe a field
being typed in. Dropping a selection is legitimate in exactly one case — the
selected thing is no longer there. Lists painted on the timer go through
`setHTML`, which writes only when the markup actually changed, so a row under
the pointer is not replaced mid-click.

## A terminal, and the files behind it (`/term`)

The fleet app's shell has a page of its own: `/term`, opened from *Fleet →
Shell → Full screen*, in a **tab**. It is the same session as the panel — the
same emulator, the same driver — with the whole screen instead of two thirds of
it, a key row for what a phone's keyboard does not have (Esc, Tab, sticky Ctrl
and Alt, arrows, `^c`), copy and paste both ways, and **Files**: browse the
machine, download, upload, make a folder. The emulator is a real one: the
alternate screen, scroll regions, 24-bit colour, mouse reporting and a size
pushed to the pty, which is what `btop` and `vim` need to draw at all — and the
screen is a **canvas**, because a grid of text in the DOM drifts the moment one
glyph falls back to a font with a different advance. Selection and the
scrollback (the wheel, Shift-PageUp/PageDown) are the screen's own; a screen
reader gets the same text from an off-screen mirror. All of it under the `shell` right and
nothing more, because an operator who can open a shell there can already read
and write those files by typing. Detail:
[`Docs/Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet).

## What a page holds, and how it stays in step

A page used to ask for its whole world on every read and replace what it held
with whatever came back. Three things were wrong with that, and all three show
up as the same symptom — **a page that suddenly has nothing on it**:

| What went wrong | What it looks like | What answers it |
|---|---|---|
| an error body assigned over held state (`apiJson` answers a 502 with a body too, and its body has no machines in it) | every list empties | a read hands back **nothing** when the answer is not one, and the page keeps what it had |
| the whole ledger re-sent because one job finished | slow, and on a relay most of what a console costs | named **sections**, sent only when their content moved |
| the node updating itself under an open page | questions the new node no longer answers, then an empty page | every answer names the **build**; a mismatch reloads the page once |

The revision of a section is a **checksum of the section itself**, not a counter
bumped at every mutation. A counter has to be bumped from every place that
writes, and the one place that forgets is a section that silently stops
updating — the worst failure, because everything still appears to work.

`GET /api/fleet/state?proto=2&have=managed:1a2b3c4d,jobs:00ff00ff` answers
`{proto, build, full, revs, sections, log, log_seq}`. A page that sends no
`proto` gets the flat snapshot it has always got, with `proto` and `build` added
beside it — **two versions at once**, which is the case a version number exists
for. A claim that cannot be read is answered in full: always correct, only ever
slower, which is the right way round for something a browser sends.

Two pages read this way, and for the same reason. The fleet page holds the
ledger — the machines, the jobs, the capabilities, the facts. The chat page
holds the social half — the contacts, the groups, this node's own name — which
is the same answer on nearly every read and used to be re-sent whole beside the
one message that had arrived. What is *already* incremental stays outside the
sections and always travels: the messages, asked for by sequence, and the fleet
log, asked for by line. A checksum buys nothing on something that is only ever
sent once.

Three smaller reads go the same way for the first reason alone — the deploy
keys, the networks an invite may be minted in, and the machines the context
selector switches between. None of them is large; each was a list taken
straight out of whatever body came back, so one read that failed removed a
choice the node still had. The selector is the plainest case: lose it and there
is no way left on the page to reach the other machines.

The rail says *Not answering* while reads are failing. That is a different and
much smaller claim than an empty list, and it is the true one.

### The change stream resumes

`/api/events` gives every change an `id:`. The browser reconnects on its own and
sends the last id back in `Last-Event-ID`; without honouring it, everything that
moved while the connection was down is simply dropped — and a page that missed a
change is a page showing yesterday. An id from another run of the console (one
naming a sequence this one has never reached) is clamped rather than obeyed: a
stream told to start ahead of the truth never speaks again.

The `ready` event carries the build and the protocol version, which is where a
page most often first notices that the node restarted into a new version — the
stream reconnects by itself after exactly that.

## Managing another node from this console

When the fleet app is running and at least one node has granted the `manage`
capability, a **context selector** appears in the top bar. Choosing it asks for
**that node's console password** — the grant opens the channel, the password
opens the session — and then the whole console talks about that node: overview,
peers, transports, apps, configuration, trace.

A node that also granted `passwordless` asks for nothing: there, the grant *is*
the second key. That right exists for machines nobody can type a password on —
the ones this operator provisioned, whose password was generated on first start
and printed to a log on a box with no screen. Taking it back on that node ends
the session immediately. See [`Docs/Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet).

Technically the page adds an `X-NMesh-Node: <id>` header to every call; the
console relays it over the mesh, and the target node **replays it against its
own console** over loopback. Nothing is reimplemented: same operations, same
caps, same lockout. Full detail, bounds and refused paths:
[`Docs/Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet).

What travels is a **control frame** — one request, one answer, over the channel
described in [`../Architecture/control-plane.md`](../Architecture/control-plane.md).
So what an operator may do to a node they manage is a list that node keeps about
itself, one declared reach per operation, rather than a denylist of URL prefixes
that every new route joined by default.

**Everything that list holds can be done from here**, and getting there took two
mechanisms rather than a wider permission:

- **What takes longer than the relay can hold travels as a job.** Installing a
  release is four hundred seconds and the relay carries fifteen, so the work
  stays there and a *ticket* comes back: `jobs.start` runs it, `jobs.poll` says
  what became of it. The page does not have to know — a node refusing a call
  because it is too long says so with a shape, and `CHANNEL.call` re-asks as a
  job by itself. A button simply takes as long as the thing behind it.
- **What a node *trusts* is a second grant.** Pinning a signing key, installing
  a release, minting an invitation, holding a private key — those are not "drive
  this console", and folding them into `manage` would have made one grant mean
  two things. They need the fleet's **`govern`** capability as well, asked for
  and taken back like every other. Without it a console reaches every read and
  every operational write, and none of those; with it, there is nothing left
  that has to be done at the machine.

- **Files go the same way.** A download used to be an `<a href download>` and an
  upload a four-megabyte body — the first cannot carry the header that says
  which node is being driven, the second does not fit the relay. Both are
  operations now (`transfer`), carried a chunk per frame, so publishing an app
  or downloading a package happens on the machine the console is pointed at.

A page never has to know which is which: it reads the far node's catalogue and
draws what that node will answer. And a window opened *beside* it — the package
card, the node card — carries the same context in its address and says so in its
own strip.

### What makes the switch reliable

Three things, because "which machine am I on" is the one question a console must
never get wrong.

- **The switch is atomic, not eventual.** Every context carries an *epoch*, and
  a request records the epoch it was made in. A reply whose epoch has moved is
  dropped rather than painted: without it, a slow answer from the machine you
  just left lands under the name of the one you just entered — and with
  auto-refresh off, it stays there. Everything the page was holding (the state
  snapshot, the throughput window, the map's names, the transport form, the
  configuration fields, what the apps offer, the trace poll) is dropped at the
  same moment, by a subscription rather than by a list somebody has to remember
  to extend.

- **The strip is on every page.** It is written once (`ui.CTX_BAR`) and embedded
  by the console, chat and fleet, so the three cannot say it differently. It
  stays for as long as you are driving another machine, `Sign out` hands the
  remote session back and returns here, and the context survives a page reload —
  checked against the console that holds the session before it is believed.

- **A node that stops answering hands the context back.** A session it dropped,
  or a relay with no session left to it, ends the context at once and says so;
  silence is marked in the strip (*not answering*) and ends it after three tries
  in a row. Before, a page driving a node that had gone answered nothing, looked
  alive and had to be reloaded by hand. And each shared view — the channel's
  catalogue, the node card — drops what it holds *itself* when the context
  changes, so a switch on `/chat` or `/node` resets as thoroughly as one on the
  console.

- **What cannot be driven remotely says so, rather than failing.** A managed
  node refuses `/api/fleet/`, `/api/remote/` and `/api/chat/` by design (a
  managed node is not a jump host; the proxy must not drive the proxy; somebody
  else's conversations were never part of managing their machine). The page
  knows the same list, so those calls are never *addressed* to it — and the
  strip on `/chat` and `/fleet` says in words that the app runs here. For
  everything on the control plane the page does not need a list at all: it reads
  the far node's catalogue (`control.catalogue`, filtered by who is asking) and
  draws the buttons that node will actually answer. A view
  mounted inside one of those apps asks this node too: *"what is my link to this
  person"* is a different question depending on who "my" is, and answering it
  from the machine being managed would be a confidently wrong answer rather than
  an error.

## The page is told, it does not ask

The console used to read the node on a timer. At two seconds a link that came
up was invisible for two seconds; at a tenth of a second it was two hundred
questions a minute answered "nothing". `GET /api/events` inverts it: the node
says when something moved, and the page reads only then.

Driving another node, the same information arrives by asking rather than by
listening: the relay carries one bounded call and its answer, never a socket
held open, so the page asks `control.changes` on its cadence and repaints on
what moved. Same topics, same handlers — where it used to be told nothing at all
and fall back to a timer.

**Only that something moved** — never what. The frame names topics
(`links`, `nodes`, `names`, `reach`) and the page reads the state it already
knows how to read. A second description of a node travelling down a second
channel is two things to keep in step.

**Structure, not measurement.** A link that came up either did or did not, and
wants to be instant. Latency, jitter, throughput and load move constantly and by
tiny amounts, so they stay on the interval — a repaint per measurement says
nothing new and costs the whole list. The number in the top bar is therefore
*that* interval, and the dot beside it says whether the stream is live — **amber
when the interval is off**, because streaming with no cadence is only half live:
links still arrive and every number stands still, and a green dot there would be
the console lying by omission.

**A frame, not a firehose.** One stream wakes at most ten times a second
(`_STREAM_FRAME`), and every change inside a frame is answered by one repaint on
the page (`EVENTS.FRAME`), each handler running once however many topics named
it. Forty link events between two reads is one message naming one topic.

**Bounds.** At most `_MAX_STREAMS` open at once (each is a thread of the
console's server while a page is open) and the hub's topic table is capped. A
comment goes down the wire every `_STREAM_PING` seconds: it keeps an idle
connection from being reaped, and it is how a stream finds out the page
reloaded — nobody closed that socket on this side, and it is the write that
discovers it. Stopping the console unhooks the node and releases whoever is
waiting.

**Local by construction.** A stream is a connection held open, and the fleet
relay carries one bounded request and its answer. Under a remote context the
endpoint answers **409** and the page keeps its cadence, saying so rather than
waiting on a stream that will never speak.

**Every page, one control.** `/fleet` used to carry the interval's markup with
nothing behind it while polling at a rate of its own; a control that does
nothing is worse than no control, so it drives that page's poll too. The
**shell** is the exception and is on no cadence at all: its read is *held* by
the console until the pty has something to say (`&wait=1`), and the page asks
again the moment it is answered. A terminal is not a status page, the interval
can be set to zero, and a timer would only ever be a floor on how long a
keystroke takes to come back.

**A refresh writes the values, it does not redraw the page.** A view whose
numbers always change is a view `setHTML` cannot help — it only skips a write
when *nothing* moved. So a view draws its **shape** once (which rows exist,
which buttons are drawn) and the cadence writes only the numbers into the markup
already on screen. Before that, at one second apart, every fold a reader had
opened sprang shut, the text they were selecting vanished, and the row under
their finger was a different element by the time the tap landed. A row is keyed
by the link it stands for, so the same link keeps the same element; and only a
node that is actually **gone** folds a row back — a second link dropping for one
tick used to fold it, and it stayed folded.

**Every view, one cadence.** The interval drives a *list* of jobs, not one. It
used to be one, so a view showing a moving number was on the cadence only if
somebody had remembered to call it from inside that job — and the node card was
not, so its latency, jitter, loss and volume stood still for as long as it was
open, on every page that mounts it and on `/node`, which armed no cadence at
all. A view registers itself (`REFRESH.on`) and cannot be the one that was
forgotten; every page arms it (`REFRESH.mount`), including the ones with no
control of their own — the interval is a per-browser preference and applies
wherever numbers move.

What that costs is kept honest at both ends. The card re-reads only the *live*
rows on a tick — every number that moves is in them; the address book and the
identity key are not, and a structural change re-reads those too, along with
what the apps make of the identity. And a card nobody is looking at asks for
nothing: a closed dialog keeps its contents in the page, so being connected is
not the same as being read.

## Watching the mesh live

### The whole network, on one page (`/network`)

Every other page answers a question about *one* thing, when asked. This one
answers **what is happening, anywhere, right now**, and keeps answering it — a
screen to leave open beside the work rather than a panel to switch to. Reached
from *Network → Watch it live*, and opened in a tab of its own because that is
what it is for.

Two live regions and one switch:

- **The board** — every node this console can see, in one list: this node first,
  then the machines the fleet collects logs from, then everything in the routing
  table. One row per identity, whichever ledger it came from, carrying where it
  stands (connected, silent, known), its transport and round trip, and — in the
  one place a reader looks for it — whether its log reaches here at all.
- **The stream** — the lines those nodes are saying, oldest at the top, arriving
  as they arrive. It follows the newest line until you scroll up, and then says
  how many it is holding back rather than yanking you forward every second.
- **The switch** says whether this node is keeping a log of itself, and is the
  only thing on the page that changes anything. Nothing starts recording because
  a page was opened: a console that began keeping a record of who this node
  talks to because a tab was left open would be the wrong default, whoever left
  it open. Stopping asks first, because stopping **drops** what was kept.

Three things worth knowing about what it shows:

- **The filters are a view of what the page is holding, not a subscription.**
  Both halves are followed by sequence number, and a cursor consumes what it
  passes — so a filtered subscription would have already thrown away the lines a
  *new* filter wanted, with nothing to say it had. Changing a filter therefore
  costs no call and loses nothing.
- **It answers for this console**, not for whichever node the console next door
  is driving. The collected rings are on this machine; a managed node's idea of
  the network is a different question with the same wording.
- **The numbers say what they count.** "Nodes connected" and "links held" are
  two figures, not one under two labels — a node may hold several links — and
  "in view" means in the page's own bounded buffer, which is also what the
  board's per-node counts are drawn from, so the two cannot disagree.

The page reads `logs.since`, `logs.status`, `node.state` and the fleet's
`logs_stream` / `logs_machines`. All of them read structures this machine
already holds: a page following forty machines is not forty machines' worth of
traffic.

- **The map** (Overview → Topology) opens large in one click — on the map
  itself, or on **Expand**. The expanded map labels each link with its medium,
  latency and loss, thickens the stroke with the volume carried, turns a link
  losing probes amber, and lists the links beside it with their latency curve.
  It redraws on every tick while it is open.
  - **It pans and zooms**: drag to move, wheel or pinch to zoom (around the
    cursor or the two fingers, not the centre), `+` / `−` / `⤢` at the bottom
    right, `+` `-` `0` on the keyboard, double-click to see everything again.
    The zoom survives the refresh: the view does not flatten itself every two
    seconds under your finger.
  - Labels keep a **constant size on screen** whatever the zoom — zooming
    spreads the mesh out instead of inflating the text — and the map **drops
    detail when it is too small for it**: link legends first, then names. On a
    phone the overview is therefore a clean topology; names come back as you
    zoom, and a tap on a node selects it anyway.
  - On a narrow screen the link list is not removed: it moves **below** the
    drawing.
  - **One selection, shared with the list beside it.** Clicking a node on the
    map selects it in the link list, and clicking a row in the list highlights
    the node and its edges on the map. The selected row carries a **Details**
    button — selecting is not opening, so a tap that only meant to point at
    something does not throw a dialog over the map.
  - **Past what this node can see** (the panel's *Beyond this node*). The
    drawing stops at this node's neighbours and the sessions it routes, because
    that is everything it can *measure*. While the map is open, the machines
    this console manages that granted `links` report what **they** are connected
    to — and say so again whenever it changes, rather than when something here
    happens to ask.

    What comes back is a **claim**, kept apart on every axis: its own outer
    ring, a dashed hollow dot, an edge labelled "said by …" rather than with a
    latency nobody here measured, its own rows in the panel, and each machine's
    age beside it.

    It is a picture of **now**. Fleet keeps a freshness book rather than a
    history: a machine that has not confirmed its links in the last 45 seconds
    has *no* links on the map, not old ones. Closing the map stops the follows
    within seconds and drops what was held — so the map is never showing
    something nobody has confirmed, and a map nobody has open costs nothing at
    all.

    The state lives in the fleet app, not in the page: a reload does not empty
    the map, and two tabs do not each go asking every machine. It is memory
    only — a claim about somebody else's neighbours is not something to keep
    across a restart — and *Drop what was reported* empties it, after which
    whatever is still true is reported again a moment later.

    Only machines that granted `links` are asked, and only fleet offers any of
    this. The three questions the map asks fleet (`map_links`, `map_targets`,
    `map_overlay`) **do not travel**: together they hand over the list of
    machines this node manages, so they are answered only for the console an
    operator is sitting at.
  - **What an app knows about a node is drawn beside it.** Fleet says "managed",
    "controls this node", "log" — its own words, rendered by a map that holds no
    idea of what any of them mean.
- **Which link carries the traffic is chosen, not stumbled on.** A node reached
  over two media at once may have one of them losing every probe. The score that
  picks between them multiplies the address score by `(1 − loss)⁴`, so a tenth
  of the probes lost costs more than any latency difference and a link nothing
  comes back from is never chosen while anything else exists.
- **And a link that answers nothing is cut.** Scoring it at zero keeps traffic
  off it; it does not get rid of it, and a half-open connection stays listed and
  counted for ever while the far end — which has no such link — dials us to get
  one. Four unanswered probes in a row (over a minute of one-way silence) and
  the link goes. Judged per link, so a node reached over `tcp` and `udp` loses
  only the medium that went quiet; judged on a **run**, not the lifetime share,
  because a link that worked for an hour and then died never shows a high share;
  and any answer resets it, including one too late to be timed. The node keeps
  the identity and its addresses, so the next maintenance pass dials it again.
  See [`../Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md).
- **Connected nodes are one row each**, whatever the number of links. A node you
  hold five links with is one row: its name, how many links, the lowest latency,
  and the jitter **of that fastest link** — the jitter of a link nobody uses
  answers no question. Unfold the row to see each link. The unfolded state is
  per table (active and known are independent) and survives the refresh; a row
  folds back only when the node it belongs to is gone.
  The count beside the heading, and the one on the Network tab, are therefore
  **nodes**; the page is paged by node too, so a node's links can never be split
  across two pages and show it twice.
- **A node's card** is the *same* everywhere: the console's dialog, a panel
  beside the conversation in chat, fleet's sheet, or the `/node#<id>` page in a
  window or a tab. It offers only what the installed apps make possible
  (`Docs/AppAPI/guide`) and hides the button that leads back where you came
  from: from chat, no "Message"; from fleet, no fleet button.

  It is laid out as the three questions somebody opens it with, in that order.

  1. **Who is this, and what are we to each other?** The name, the id under it,
     and at most two badges — how the node is reached, and whether its key is
     known. Then the relationship in **sentences**: in your contacts, what you
     hold on it, what it holds on *this* node. Those last two are independent
     and are never one summary; "it can drive this console" is the reason to
     open the card at all, and it used to be a row two thirds of the way down a
     table.
  2. **Is the link any good?** The links are **drawn** before they are listed:
     one wire per link, thicker with what it carries, amber when it is losing
     probes — and saying so in words, because colour is never the only signal.
     A node reached over `tcp` **and** `udp` at once is the normal case here and
     a list of rows never showed it as one shape. Under the drawing, the four
     numbers of the *fastest* link (the jitter of a link nobody uses answers no
     question), then one expandable row per link: scheme, endpoint, latency,
     uptime — opening onto its endpoints as the medium sees them, its direction,
     what it carried, its probes, and the transport's **own** counters under
     their own names.
  3. **Everything else, folded**, with its count in the title so nobody has to
     open a fold to find out it is empty: **the state of every known address**
     (in service, unreachable, never tried) with a **Retry** on each row, and
     the identity and session facts.

  **Actions** follow the same rule. The one or two anybody came for stay visible
  — message them, open them in fleet, ping them — and the rest go behind the
  **⋯**: copy the identity, open it in its own window, add to contacts, mint an
  invitation on its behalf (with the `invite` right), retry every address,
  forget the node. An overflow menu is where secondary actions belong and where
  primary ones go to be never found, so nothing anybody needs often is put in
  it.

  The card **repaints when a link moves**, off the same change stream as the
  rest of the console — with a floor of its own on top of the stream's frame,
  because one repaint here is five requests — and never while the ⋯ is open:
  replacing the panel under the finger that opened it is worse than being a beat
  late.

  - **Requesting fleet rights** asks *which ones*, with what each allows:
    "access" on its own is not a question anyone can answer. Nothing is granted
    by asking — the request lands on the other machine and a human there decides,
    and may grant less than was asked.
- **Settings → This browser** keeps what belongs to this screen and not to the
  node: the theme, the auto-refresh interval, and how a link into another app
  opens (a window, a tab, or by screen size). Two machines connected to the same
  console each keep their own; nothing is sent anywhere.

### Network → Reachability

Reachability is organised as **one block per medium**. Unfolding a medium gives
two views, chosen with a Status / Settings pair at the top of the block:

- **Status** — what it carries (links, average latency, volume, ports), what it
  says about the outside world (public IP and the address a STUN server saw, for
  `tcp` and `udp`), its listeners (adding and removing), and its own commands.
- **Settings** — its settings, rendered from what the medium declares
  (checkboxes, multi-choice lists, bounded numbers, free fields). Applied to the
  live node and then written to the configuration file; a refused value stays on
  screen with its reason and never reaches the file.

Splitting the two is why the public IP is no longer on the main page: it is a
fact about **one transport**, and a page that shows it at the top teaches that
the node is its TCP/IP address. Everything tied to a medium now lives inside
that medium's block. The **relay-capable** badge sits with `tcp` and `udp` for
the same reason — today that is a **presentation** choice only: the internal
logic is unchanged and still node-wide, and measuring relay capability per
medium is backend work that has not been done.

- The **udp** block therefore carries the port, starting and stopping the
  socket, hole punching, NAT keepalive and LAN discovery: all of that rides on
  that one socket. These settings used to sit in a neutrally named "Listeners &
  addressing" card, when they are TCP/IP.
- **`retry interval`** (tcp and udp): how often to redial a known node we hold
  no link to, on each of its addresses. `0` — the default — turns it off:
  nothing is retried until someone needs a route.
- **`priority`** (every transport): from −254 to 254, what this node thinks of
  that medium. Shipped as `udp` **10**, `tcp` **0**, `spool` **−50**.
- **The "Choosing between a node's addresses" slider** (Reachability card) says
  how priority and measured latency share the decision: hard left, only speed
  counts; hard right, only the chosen medium. The line below shows **the
  resulting order**, computed by the node — the console does not reimplement the
  rule.
- **Dynamic addressing** (Reachability card, off by default) moves a live link
  to another address of the **same** node when it **scores** better — a LAN
  address rather than the public one, IPv6 rather than IPv4, or simply the
  medium we prefer. The candidate is really dialled and probed before we move,
  and we only move on a clear gain; the loser is closed either way.

What belongs to no medium stays apart: the **Addressing** card says what this
node claims about itself (advertised addresses, local IPs, schemes).

The **Membership** card says what this node can prove about *itself*: where it
stands, who admitted it, what its chain anchors on, and — the part that matters —
how long its certificate has left. Standing is one of four words, and three of
them look identical in every other field on the card:

- **Member of a network** — a chain up to a root somebody else signed.
- **This network's own root** — this node founded a network and has admitted
  members, so its own root is a root for them.
- **Membership has run out** — it still trusts the anchor it was admitted under,
  but can no longer build a chain to it.
- **Not a member of any network** — it has joined nothing yet.

A membership lasts a year and renews itself a month before it runs out (see
[`../Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md)); should that ever
fail, this shows as a countdown rather than as peers quietly refusing to
authenticate a week later. Once it hits zero the only way back in is a fresh
invitation — a lapsed membership is never re-signed.

The last two standings mean **nothing on the network will authenticate this
node**, and every symptom of that turns up somewhere else looking like a broken
network: links that will not form, a topology that stays empty, dials that all
report no answer. So they are said in a bar across the top of whatever page you
are on, with what to do next, rather than waiting to be found on this card. On a
node with nothing connected, Overview says the same thing as an ordered list of
the steps — and both disappear the moment a link authenticates.

*Add a node* opens with **Invite a node** — one string, whichever way round the
two machines are. It carries the inviter's own address when it has one, a relay
to reach it through when it does not, and the same single-use code either way,
so there is nothing to choose before inviting anybody and no second exchange.
Under it, *Use one* takes any of them in one field: an invitation, or a block
pasted from an older node. Below that, and folded away: the manual exchange for
when neither end can dial the other (the fallback that needs no address to work
at all), and the primitives the rest is built on.

**And it need not be this node's network.** *Into which node's network* lists
every node that granted this one the fleet `invite` capability — because the
node which will **honour** a code is the node that mints it, so inviting
somebody into a mesh reached through another machine means asking that machine.
Choosing one sends the ask over the mesh and shows what comes back, once.

**Anchors, and taking a membership back** is its own fold, and deliberately so:
nothing in it adds a node. It holds the two ways trust is withdrawn, and they
are not the same act:

- **Take back a membership** revokes a node *this one admitted*. The statement is
  signed, so it can never reach somebody else's members; it travels the mesh,
  every node that hears it stops accepting that node's chain, and the links it
  holds here end immediately rather than at the next handshake. Only an
  invitation lets it back in.
- **Anchors this node trusts** drops a root. That is local and only local — a
  root's certificate is signed by itself, so there is nobody above it to take it
  back, and nothing is announced. Dropping one stops *every* membership that
  chains up to it from authenticating here. This node's own id is not in the
  list: a node that is not its own root can present nothing at all.

**Nodes under suspicion** is what the applications and the core have reported.
Being in the network is not being trusted: each app sets its own idea of too
much and reports the sender, and this node decides what the reports add up to
(the thresholds are `abuse_suspect` / `abuse_hostile` / `abuse_halflife` in
`nmesh.conf`). A node past the first threshold **stops being served, silently** —
it is told nothing, so it learns as late as possible — and past the second it is
refused a fresh link too. Complaints fade on their own, so a node that
misbehaved once and then behaved comes back with nobody doing anything;
`Clear` drops everything held against one, because every entry here is a
judgement made under pressure by software and some of them will be wrong about
somebody's node. The card is hidden while the table is empty, which is its
normal state.

**Refused handshakes** is the card to open when nothing will connect. A node
drops anything it cannot verify — that is the design — but a mesh whose nodes
all refuse each other looks, from outside, exactly like a mesh nobody is
calling: the dialler retries, every attempt dies at one of a dozen tests, and
none of them used to say a word. The card lists the tests that failed, newest
first, with how many times and which id was turned away. It is hidden while
there is nothing to show, and nothing in it is an error on its own — a stranger
dialling a closed door lands here too.

Two reasons mean the two nodes are no longer in the same network, and are worth
acting on: *the certificate chain reaches no root we trust* and *no certificate
chain was presented*. The rest are noise or a bug worth reporting.

These numbers come from the transport itself (`endpoints()` / `stats()`), so a
new medium appears here with no change to the console. See
[`Docs/Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md).

## Every route answers

`do_GET` and `do_POST` both run through `_answering`, which turns any unhandled
exception into **500** with a fixed sentence. Without it an exception unwinds
through `handle_one_request`, which sends no status and closes the socket: a
page sees a dropped connection rather than an error, and whatever spinner it
drew stays on screen for ever. The text is fixed on purpose — an exception's
message is this machine's internals, and some routes answer before anybody has
proved anything.

The page side is the same rule: `PACKAGES.ask` returns a failure rather than
throwing, so a fetch that dies draws an error card instead of leaving a
skeleton. A spinner with no end is a bug report nobody can act on.

## Security model

- **TLS**, self-signed (ECDSA P-256), fingerprint printed at startup.
- **Password** generated on the first run, stored only as a **salted scrypt
  hash** (`console.cred`, mode 0600). Never in the clear on disk.
- **Session** authenticated by a **Bearer token** (`Authorization` header) **or
  a session cookie**. Login sets an `nmesh_session` cookie: `HttpOnly` (the
  page's JS cannot read it → an XSS does not exfiltrate it), `SameSite=Strict`
  (never sent on a cross-site request → **no CSRF surface**, the property that
  once justified having no cookie at all), and `Secure` under TLS. Both paths
  validate the **same** token (sliding expiry, one hour of inactivity); the
  cookie exists so that **refreshing the page no longer forces a re-login**. It
  is a session cookie (no `Max-Age`): it survives a refresh, disappears when the
  browser closes, and the server bounds the token's lifetime regardless.
- **Lockout** against brute force after several failed logins.
- **Loopback bind by default**; exposing it on the network is an explicit
  choice.
- **Strict CSP** (`default-src 'self'`), same-origin assets, no external
  resource (no CDN), a cap on request size.
- The HTTP server runs in a dedicated thread and **marshals** every interaction
  with the node onto the asyncio loop: the node's state is never touched outside
  the loop's thread.

## Somebody is here

Every authenticated request tells the node a page is open, and a change stream
held open says so for as long as it is held. The node uses it to decide what it
spends on itself — multi-link operation is a probe ten times a second per
bundled link and is not worth paying for in an empty room
([`Docs/Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md)).

Every route counts, not just `/api/state`: the chat and fleet pages never read
the node's state, so waking on that one route read a console open on chat as an
empty room. A call a peer **replays** against this console through the fleet's
`manage` right does not count — it is a page on their machine, and waking this
node must not be something the network can do to it.

## Files served

| Route | Role |
|---|---|
| `/`, `/style.css`, `/app.js` | the console |
| `/chat`, `/chat.css`, `/chat.js` | the chat app, if enabled |
| `/fleet`, `/fleet.css`, `/fleet.js` | the fleet app, if enabled |
| `/network`, `/network.css`, `/network.js` | the whole network, live: the board of every node this console can see, and the log lines arriving from the ones it collects |
| `/node`, `/node.css`, `/node.js` | the node card as a page (`/node#<id>`) |
| `/package`, `/package.css`, `/package.js` | one signed package, described (`/package#<record id>`) — mounted by Settings → Updates, Apps → Find an app and the node card, and served on its own for the window or tab an operator prefers |
| `/theme.js` | sets the chosen theme **before the first paint** (blocking in `<head>`) |
| `/favicon.svg` | the icon; without it every load takes a 404 on `/favicon.ico` |

Everything is served from the same origin with no external resource: the console
works on a machine with no internet.

## API (all JSON, Bearer or cookie auth except login)

Two doors, and one of them is the future: **`POST /api/control`** carries the
management plane — one frame naming an operation, one frame back — and the rest
of the table is what has not moved onto it yet. A route that *has* moved is a
thin adapter over the same operation rather than a second implementation, so
both stay true at once. The ledger of what is where, and what a refusal means:
[`../Architecture/control-plane.md`](../Architecture/control-plane.md).

A refusal is one shape everywhere: the status from a code out of a closed set,
`{"error": "…"}`, and whatever structure the operation attached (the settings
form gets its `rejected` list). Where a route used to answer `{"ok": false}`, it
now answers `{"error": …}` with the same status.

| Method | Route            | Role                                           |
|---------|------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| POST    | `/api/control`   | the control channel: `{v:1, id, op, params}` → `{v:1, id, ok, result}` or `{ok:false, code, error, detail?}`. With `X-NMesh-Node` the **same frame** is relayed to that node and its answer handed back — the status then describes this console (200: it answered) and the frame describes that node |
| POST    | `/api/login`     | `{password}` → `{token}` + sets the `nmesh_session` cookie |
| POST    | `/api/logout`    | revokes the token (Bearer or cookie) and clears the cookie |
| POST    | `/api/control`   | …and `node.speedtest`, the one reading that **loads** a link rather than watching it: a bounded burst of padding echoed back by the peer, answered only over a direct authenticated link. It runs as a job (ten seconds at most), so the node card's **Speed test** button works the same whether the console is on this machine or driving another |
| GET     | `/api/state`     | snapshot (id, peers, routing, throughput, load, **addressing**, `handshake_refusals`, `trust`) — `node.state`. Also `broken`: the sections that could not be built, empty on a healthy node. One diagnostic raising used to take the whole document with it, and with it every page of the console; now it degrades alone, says so, and writes its traceback to the node's log |
| POST    | `/api/control`   | …and **files**, on that same channel: `transfer.offer`/`put`/`commit` sends a named set of byte streams a chunk per frame, `transfer.fetch`/`take` reads one back. What the bytes are *for* is a **kind**, with its own reach and its own ceiling — `package` to any console holding `manage`, `app` and `release` behind `govern` |
