| POST    | `/api/packages/subscribe` | `{id, on, auto?, quorum?}` — watch the **package** that record names, and say how many of the signing keys this operator chose must agree on the code first. Stopping also accepts the subscription's own id: it is all the watching table has when this node holds no record for the package |
| GET     | `/api/keys`      | publisher keys held here, offers made from here and still standing, offers waiting for an answer — `keys.overview`, and the **only** key operation a console managing this node may reach: a passphrase is typed at the machine that will hold the key |
| POST    | `/api/keys/create` / `import` / `forget` | make a key under a passphrase, adopt an existing file by path, or drop one from this node (`forget` needs `confirm:true`) — `keys.create` / `keys.adopt` / `keys.forget`, all local only |
| POST    | `/api/keys/offer` | `{node, key_id, passphrase, confirm:true, label?}` — offer the key to another node. Signed by the key itself; **nothing secret travels yet** |
| POST    | `/api/keys/accept` / `refuse` | `{offer_id, …}` — accept (a passphrase of your own, `confirm:true`) and the secret is sealed to a fresh KEM key the acceptance carries, or refuse and it lapses |
| GET     | `/api/update/check` | asks GitHub → `{current, latest, available, notes, source, branch, can_apply, blocked}`. `source` is `release` (the published releases) or `branch` (`src/version.py` at the branch `update_branch` names) — `releases.check`; GitHub not answering comes back *as the answer*, beside the version that is running |
| POST    | `/api/update/apply` | `{version, confirm}` — installs that **exact** version; a mismatch with what GitHub offers is refused, and a branch that moved past it is refused again when its tree arrives — `releases.apply`, local only |
| POST    | `/api/restart`   | `{confirm:true}` — restarts the node: it exits when a supervisor will start it again, and otherwise re-execs itself. **409** with the reason when neither is possible — `node.restart`, and under a remote context it is the *managed* node that restarts |
| GET     | `/api/config`    | the node's settings: current values, editable or not, problems in the file — `config.get` |
| POST    | `/api/config`    | `{settings:{…}}` — writes the file; one refused value writes **nothing**, and the refusal names which — `config.save`. A value may not span two lines: the file is one `name = value` per line, so a newline in one would write a *second setting* rather than a longer value |
| GET     | `/api/trace`     | `?events=1` → state + totals per message type (and the events) — `trace.status` |
| POST    | `/api/trace`     | `{action:start\|stop\|clear, seconds?, events?}` — requested bounds are **capped**, never obeyed (the trace owns the ceiling; the plane clamps to that same constant) — `trace.set` |
| GET     | `/api/trace/export` | the whole trace as JSON (download) — `trace.export` |
| —       | (plane only)     | `logs.status`, `logs.set`, `logs.query`, `logs.since`, `logs.sources` — the node's own log ring, off until asked, bounded in megabytes ([`../Architecture/logging.md`](../Architecture/logging.md)) |
| —       | (plane only)     | `alerts.list`, `alerts.ack`, `alerts.drop` — the notice board, always on ([`../Architecture/alerts.md`](../Architecture/alerts.md)). It also rides `node.state` as `alerts`, so a page that reads the snapshot already has it |
| POST    | `/api/apps/grant` | `{id, capability, granted}` — gives or takes back one grant from a built-in app — `apps.grant` |
| POST    | `/api/password`  | `{current, new}` — changes the password; **the current one is required** even with a valid session |

The **Expert — addressing & transports** section shows every concrete URI
advertised to reach the node (wildcard listens expanded over each local IP), the
listening URIs, the local IPs, and the registered transports (● = active). The
snapshot exposes `advertised`, `listen`, `local_ips`, `transports`, `listening`.

A row in **Known nodes** reads `authenticated`, `key known`, `no key` — or
**never answers**, which is a different fact rather than a worse one and is the
one that explains a row that never changes: that id has never once answered a
lookup of ours, so the node has stopped asking after it, stopped dialling it and
stopped naming it to others. It says nothing about the node itself; anybody who
can reach it still names it, and we try again every few minutes. The usual cause
is a machine that changed identity — the old id is still being gossiped, and
answers nowhere. The snapshot carries it as `silent` on the routing row; see
`Docs/Architecture/routing.md`.

In a node's card (**Network → Peers**, the **Details** button), **Forget node**
removes that node from the local routing table and cuts its session if it is
live. It is **not a ban**: automatic merging into the routing table on receiving
an authenticated PONG (and neighbourhood maintenance) can learn the node again
if it is still reachable — see `Docs/Architecture/gotchas.md`.

The interface's **Apps (DHT)** section publishes an application (pick files → an
identifier shared over the mesh) and fetches one by its identifier (files
verified, then downloadable). See `Docs/AppSharing/guide`.

**Find an app** does not list anything. A store is a list, and a list is a thing
to flood — keeping one clean needs an authority nobody on a decentralised mesh
can be. So it asks: you type part of a name and signed records filed under it
come back from the package directory, each carrying its author's key. Opening one
gives the same page a node release opens (`/package#<id>`): what it is, what it
says about itself, who else signed the same code, and download / install / watch.
The **Installed** tab is still a list, because what is on this disk is a finite
set this operator put there. See `Docs/AppStore/guide` and
`Docs/Updates/guide`.

## The built-in chat app (`/chat`)

The node ships a **default chat app**, plugged into the mesh like any other app
(data connector, `CHAT_APP_ID` section). The console surfaces it:

- the **Apps** section lists the built-in apps with an **Open** link; the rail
  keeps a permanent shortcut to it.
- the **`/chat`** sub-page serves the chat interface, hosted by **the same
  console server** (no separate port or token). It **reuses the session**: the
  `nmesh_session` cookie (same origin, `Path=/`) authenticates the sub-page
  automatically, and the Bearer token is still passed through `sessionStorage`
  (per tab, never on disk) for explicit use; opened cold with no valid session,
  the page asks for the console password and logs in by itself. A plain
  **refresh** no longer asks for anything.
- the `/api/chat/*` endpoints require the **same token** as the rest of the
  console. With no chat app attached, neither `/chat` nor `/api/chat/*` exists
  (404) and the app list is empty.

Wiring: pass a `chat_bridge` (a `src.apps.chat_web.ChatBridge` wrapping a
`ChatApp`) to `WebConsole(...)`. The `scripts/nmesh_node.py` launcher does it by
default; `--no-chat` turns it off. See `Docs/Apps/chat`.

## The built-in Fleet app (`/fleet`)

Remote management and automated deployment, served by the **same console
server** (same session, same cookie) as the chat sub-page. **Off by default**:
it can open a shell, so it is enabled explicitly (`--fleet`, or the **Enable**
button in the Apps section).

The page has six sections, split by the question being asked: **Nodes** (the
ones we control, with their status and the update / docker / shell / scan
actions), **Who controls this node** (the ones controlling us, with their rights
and the pending requests), **Docker** (containers, stacks, images and the
Portainer in front of them, on one node at a time), **Discover & deploy** (LAN
discovery and SSH provisioning), **Shell** (an interactive terminal),
**Activity** (the log).

**Groups** sit above the node list: this console's own names for sets of
machines. A node can be in several, membership lives in one list so editing from
either side is the same edit, and **Update group** runs *Update* on every node in
it — each with the docker stacks chosen for it. Nothing about a group is sent to
any node; it is a way of pointing at several at once from here.

The `/api/fleet/*` routes require the **same session** as the rest of the
console, and do not exist (404) when the app is disabled. They only allow
*asking*: the three authorisation gates live on the target node, and this
console can grant itself nothing. See [`Docs/Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet).

## Built-in apps: install, enable, disable

The **Apps** section lists the apps shipped with the node as cards — icon, name,
state (a "Running", "Stopped" or "Not installed" dot), description, then the
Install / Enable / Disable / Uninstall actions and the **Open** link
(`/api/apps/*`). Two distinct states: **installed** (the node keeps the app's
state; uninstalling **purges its encrypted drawer**) and **enabled** (the app is
plugged into the mesh right now; disabling stops it, its state survives). The
toggle is **live** — no node restart — and persisted in `<data>/apps.json`.

Each installed card also carries its **grants** as tick boxes: what that app may
ask of the node *beyond running*. They are rendered from what the node declares,
never from a list held in the page, so a grant added tomorrow appears with the
node's own words for it. Today there is one — `logs`, reading the node's log
ring: every other app's lines and the core's, not only its own. Off for every
app until an operator ticks it, and dropped when the app is uninstalled, so one
reinstalled later starts from nothing rather than from what somebody allowed the
app that used to have that name. Writing a line needs no grant: the node stamps
the source, so an app can only ever be quoted as itself.

Wiring: pass an `app_host` (a `src.app_registry.AppHost`) to `WebConsole(...)`.
`scripts/nmesh_node.py` does it; the `chat_bridge` parameter stays supported for
a runner that hosts a single app itself.

## This node's name (Settings → Identity)

Two cards. The first sets **what this node is called** — the name shown beside
its id everywhere, in the console and in every app. Saving does two things: the
node signs a new claim and announces it to the mesh straight away, and the name
is written to the configuration file when there is one.

The node keeps the name across a restart either way: it signed the claim, and
its name store holds it (see [`Docs/Pseudos/guide`](../Pseudos/guide)). What the
file adds is that the *declared* value agrees — a configuration still naming the
old node wins at the next start. If that write fails the rename still stands,
and the page says so rather than pretending otherwise.

A name the network would refuse is refused here, in the same words: at most 50
characters, nothing invisible or directional, plain spaces only. See
[`Docs/Pseudos/guide`](../Pseudos/guide).

The second card **finds a node by name**, whole or partial, ranked best-first
and answered instantly from what this node already knows. *Ask the network* adds
one directory lookup for the exact name, which reaches somebody this node has
never met. Every result carries the node id and opens the node card.

The **Connected nodes** and **Known nodes** tables filter on names too, through the
search box they already have.

## Updates (Settings → Updates)

*Settings → Diagnostics* opens with **What is running**: every background job
the node keeps, what it does, **what wakes it**, how many passes it has made and
how long it has been idle. A process CPU figure says how much work is happening
and never which part is doing it; this is the other half. Each loop declares
itself when it starts and counts its own passes — two attribute writes, measured
at about a hundred nanoseconds, and nothing on the path a packet takes. *Wakes
on* matters more than it looks: since these loops stopped polling, what each one
waits for is a real question, and the answer is read off the loop rather than
kept in a second list somebody has to remember to update.

Overview also carries **Happening now**: the last handful of things this node
did or was told — a link up or lost, a handshake refused and why, an address
dropped for answering as somebody else, its own code published. It is written
where the node already knows something happened, never per packet, and the text
is always the node's own words: nothing a peer wrote reaches it, which is what
keeps a log an adversary can write into from existing. Repeats coalesce onto one
row with a count, so a peer flapping twice a second cannot push everything else
out of view, and the card is hidden entirely while nothing has happened.

Overview's tiles say **Relayed** — of what left this node, how much was carried
for somebody else. Relaying is the one thing a node spends bandwidth on with
nothing of its own to show for it, and it used to be indistinguishable from its
own traffic in every number on the page. **CPU** and **Memory** are this
process, never the machine.

*Settings → Updates* opens by saying which version is running, because that is
the question the page exists to answer. Under it the two channels are grouped
apart, since they are independent and neither is a fallback for the other:
**getting an update** (the project's releases, and the mesh's), **who may
replace this node's code** (the publishers), and **giving one** (publishing this
node's own tree).

**From the project** carries a *Check for updates* button. If a newer GitHub release exists, it is shown with its notes; *Install*
opens a confirmation that **names the version**, and the request sent repeats
that same version — a mismatch with what GitHub offers at click time is refused
rather than installing something the operator has not seen. No update ever
happens without that click.

What is checked depends on one setting: with `update_branch` empty (the default)
it is the published releases, and with a branch named it is the `__version__`
declared in `src/version.py` at that branch — the card says which, on every
answer, since "up to date" means a different thing against each. See
[`../Setup/guide`](../Setup/guide).

A row says three separate things about a release and each says only what it
knows: **this node** is who signed it, **pinned** is whether that key is one this
node accepts *code* from — a key pinned from an app's page is on the list and is
not that — and the state on the right is what can be done with it.
A node that publishes its own code and has not pinned its own key is the ordinary
case — publishing is not trusting — and the row shows both badges rather than
leaving you to read "unpinned" against your own release. A version this node is
already running says so, whoever signed it: there is nothing to install and no
trust to decide.

**From the mesh** lists releases **published by nodes**, signed with their own identity, with no web host in the way. Publishing
signs the tree and announces it; the package itself moves only when someone asks
for it, and whoever receives it can serve the next node. You pin the publisher
keys this node accepts; a release from anyone else is shown and relayed but
never installed, and pinning a publisher is a separate decision from letting
them install while nobody is watching. **Signing keys you accept** lists what is
pinned and, per key, what its signature is accepted for: this node's code, or
only the apps it signs. This node's own code can be signed and
offered from the same page. The full mechanism — the descriptor, the
gossip, the three gates before anything touches disk:
[`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide).

Either way, installing replaces the files and then **restarts the node** so the
new code is the code that runs — but only when a service manager is there to
bring it back (`NMESH_SERVICE_MANAGED`). Without one, the node stays up and the
page says to restart it yourself rather than pretending. An unattended install
restarts on the same terms: a tree written and never started is an update that
did not happen. What keeps that from looping is a record written **before** the
node leaves — a release that installs and never becomes the running version is
retried once and then abandoned, and the log on the page says so.

### A node's packages (the node card, and `/node`)

The details window for any node carries a **Packages** section, and it is the
only place in the console where a package is tied to a machine. What it lists is
what that node signed about itself — *I hold this release and I serve it* — in
four groups:

```
Node software it publishes     Node software it serves
Apps it publishes              Apps it serves
```

"Publishes" is the same sentence plus a proof that the node also holds the key
which signed the release; "serves" is a node that has the bytes and will hand
them over. Both mean the package can be had from there. Neither is authority:
what may be installed is decided by the keys this console has pinned, never by
which list a row landed in — and only a published row carries a key to pin,
because pinning a mirror would hand the machine to whoever handed you a copy.

Each row opens the package page (version, who signed it, the mini-readme, a
download, install, and the watch toggle). *Ask the network* re-runs the lookup
wide instead of answering from what this node already holds.

### Publisher keys, and handing one to somebody else

Below the publishing form, two cards say what this node can sign with and who
else it has been shared with. **Publisher keys held here** lists each key by id
and label, whether it was made here or received and from whom, and lets an
operator create one, adopt an existing file by path, or forget one. The publish
form then picks a signer — this node's identity, or one of those keys, in which
case it asks for that file's passphrase at the moment of signing and keeps it
nowhere.

**Sharing a key** is the handover: choose a key, name the node to give it to,
unlock it, confirm. Nothing secret leaves at that point — the offer only says "I
hold this key and I offer it to you", signed *by the key itself* so the claim is
not free. On the other side the offer appears with its id, who sent it, what
they call it and how long it stands; *Accept* asks for a passphrase of the
recipient's own choosing and a confirmation, and *Refuse* drops it silently. The
secret travels only after that acceptance, sealed to a key the acceptance
carried, which is why a human saying yes is not a check but the thing that makes
the transfer possible at all. The three messages and their signatures:
[`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide).

### Restarting on demand (⋯ → *Restart this node*)

The same mechanism, asked for outright rather than by an update: the node stops
its links properly and then comes back — by exiting so a supervisor starts it
again, or, when there is no supervisor, by **re-execing itself** (`os.execv`
replaces the process image with a fresh interpreter on the same command line).
It is in the **⋯** menu because it is a node action rather than a section of
one, and because from there it is one click away wherever the operator is.

Only when *neither* route is available — no interpreter to run, or the script the
node was started from is gone — is the item disabled and the reason said
underneath; `POST /api/restart` then answers **409** with that reason rather
than "restarting" for a node that would simply stop. The page reads
`can_restart` and `restart_blocked` from `/api/state`, which is that question
asked rather than the narrower "is a service manager watching?" it used to be.

Under a remote context it reaches the *managed* node's console, which is the
point: it is that machine's service manager that answers for it. The console
returns to the local node while the other one is away.

What is replaced, what is not, and the refusal cases (container, non-writable
directory): [`../Setup/guide`](../Setup/guide).

## Node configuration (Settings → Configuration)

Every launch option is editable from the console and stored in
`<prefix>/nmesh.conf`. The form is built from what the node declares
(`GET /api/config`), never from a list hard-coded in the page.

Nothing is applied live: changes take effect **when the node restarts**, and the
page says so. A refused value writes nothing at all — one bad field does not
carry the others with it, and the console cannot produce a file the node would
refuse to start on.

`launch` (what the node runs) and `data` (where the state lives) are **shown but
not editable here**: the first belongs to whoever holds the file, the second to
the installer. The console password is not a setting.

Format, bounds and the command line > file > default precedence:
[`../Setup/guide`](../Setup/guide).

## Fast join: ticket and QR code (Network → Add a node)

A full join means moving two things by hand: an address and a code. An
**invitation** packs them into one string — 34 characters for a direct IPv4 one,
76 when it carries a relay as well — dictable, typable on a phone, scannable
from a screen.

```
CTFQA4IHEMUHTNEHTMEQGWR7AHDJXX3KLQ
```

**The ticket is the secret.** Whoever can read it can join the network until it
expires or is used once — exactly as sensitive as the invitation code it
contains. Photographing a QR code on a screen takes no ingenuity, and the
interface says so next to every ticket issued.

- **Both routes, in one string.** A confirmed address of the inviter's own —
  `world` (an inbound connection really arrived on it, never one we merely
  believe is public) or, failing that, `lan`, which is a different audience and
  the whole of what a local deployment has — *and* a relay to reach it through
  when that address does not answer. The page says which it got. A node with
  neither gets a 409 and the explanation: an invitation pointing nowhere is
  worse than none, because it fails after being shared.
- **The relay half is a rendezvous.** A relayed invitation needs a post-quantum
  key and a signature, five kilobytes — far past a QR code. So the inviter
  leaves that with the relay and the string carries the relay's address and the
  inviter's node id; the joiner then sends 40 bytes. See
  [`../Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md).
- **Lifetime chosen by the operator** (1 minute to 6 hours), bounded on the
  node: an absurd value is brought back into range, never obeyed.
- **Single use**, like every invitation code: a ticket photographed by two people
  does not let two in.
- The QR is rendered by the node, in SVG, from the ticket it has just issued.
  There is **no** endpoint that turns arbitrary text into a QR code.

### Scanning with the camera

The *Scan with camera* button uses the browser's `BarcodeDetector` — no library,
as everywhere else in this project. Where it is missing (Firefox today) the page
says so plainly and pasting stays available. The camera requires the user's
permission **and** a page in a secure context (HTTPS or localhost).

## Changing the password (Settings → Console password)

`POST /api/password` requires the **current password**, even from a valid
session. That is the property that matters: without it, a stolen session would
become permanent control of the node — the thief would change the password and
lock out the person it belongs to.

- A wrong current password counts towards the **same lockout** as the login
  page: this endpoint is not a faster way to guess.
- New password: 12 characters minimum, 256 maximum (scrypt on unbounded input is
  a denial of service), no leading or trailing spaces.
- On success, **every other session is closed**; the one that made the change
  stays open — someone changing their password because they suspect a stolen
  session must not be logged out by the very act that fixes the problem.
- The hash is replaced in memory only **after** the file is written: a failed
  write leaves the old password working rather than a node nobody can get into.

Password lost entirely → `./install.sh --reset-password` on the machine itself
(see [`../Setup/guide`](../Setup/guide)).

## Protocol trace (Settings → Protocol trace)

The throughput graph says *how much*, never *what*. The trace records what the
node really sends and receives, per message type — for questions like "why are
two idle nodes talking to each other?".

It is exactly the tool that found the `FIND_NODE` / `FOUND_NODE` loop documented
in [`../Architecture/gotchas.md`](../Architecture/gotchas.md): one table per
message type, and the answer was in the first row.

What is recorded: the instant, the direction, the message type, the size on the
wire, the TTL, and the header's source/destination ids. **Never a payload** —
not the ciphertext, not the plaintext, not a key. The header is already visible
to every relay on the path; the payload is what this project exists to protect,
and a debugging tool is no reason to write it down somewhere.

That does not make it harmless: a trace is a record of **routing metadata** —
who this node keeps company with, when, and how much. So: off by default, in
memory, bounded in number of events *and* in time, it stops on its own, and it
reaches the disk only if an operator asks (0600). The bounds the request asks
for are **capped**, not obeyed.

## What wants attention (Overview)

Above everything else on the overview, and hidden when there is nothing: one
card listing what this node noticed about itself and about the machines it talks
to — a peer whose standing crossed, a guard that swallowed a failure, a problem
an app reported about itself.

Two things make it readable rather than a wall. It is **one row per problem**
with a count ("300 times"), never one per occurrence — a board that grew a row
per event would be a page an attacker writes. And it is **always on**, unlike
the log beside it: a node cannot know in advance which problem somebody will
wish they had been told about.

*Seen* marks a row read and leaves it there, because "is it still happening?" is
a question about the count. *Dismiss* forgets it; whatever raised it raises it
again if it is still true. Nothing on this card acts on anything — it is a
sentence for a person, and what acts on a peer is the reputation book.

## The node's log (`logs.*`)

Beside the trace, and answering the other half of the question: the trace says
what crossed the wire, the log says what the node was thinking while it crossed
— which gate refused a handshake, why a loop gave up on an address, whatever an
app chose to say.

Same posture as the trace, for the same reason, and two differences that matter:

- **Nothing is kept until somebody asks**, and **stopping drops what was kept**
  rather than leaving it readable. A trace is a recording an operator asked for
  and then reads; a log ring is a by-product, and one left in memory after the
  person reading it stopped is a record this node has no business holding.
- **The bound is in megabytes**, not in lines, because that is the question an
  operator has about their machine. It is compressed a block at a time, so eight
  megabytes holds on the order of a hundred thousand lines; `logs.status`
  reports the *measured* ratio rather than a claim.

Starting the trace starts the log with it: "turn the trace on" is one thing an
operator does. `logs.set` is there for what that cannot express — sizing the
ring, or keeping the lines of a node whose packet headers you have no business
recording.

A reader asks `logs.since` with the last sequence number it saw, so a console
four hops away follows a live log over the same one-question-one-answer channel
as everything else, and is told how much it `lost` if it fell behind the ring.
That is what `/network` follows — see *Watching the mesh live* above. Full
mechanics: [`../Architecture/logging.md`](../Architecture/logging.md).

## Joining / bridging two networks

1. On the host node: **Create invitation** (or, for the primitives,
   **Generate invite code**).
2. On the invited node: paste it into **Use one** — or **Join network** with
   `tcp://host:port` + the code.
3. For mutual trust between distinct roots: each node shows its certificate via
   **Show our root certificate**; the other pastes it into **Trust certificate**.

## Embedding in your own runner

```python
from src.webconsole import WebConsole
console = WebConsole(node, host="127.0.0.1", port=8787, state_dir="./data")
console.start(loop=asyncio.get_running_loop())
...
console.stop()
```
